:"Dollar Tree, Inc. operates retail discount stores under the Dollar Tree and Dollar Tree Canada brands in the United States and Canada. The company offers consumable merchandise comprising everyday consumables, such as household paper and chemicals, food, candy, health, personal care products, and frozen and refrigerated food; variety merchandise consisting of toys, durable housewares, gifts, stationery, party goods, greeting cards, softlines, arts and crafts supplies, and other items; and seasonal goods, including Christmas, Easter, Halloween, and Valentine's Day merchandise. The company was founded in 1986 and is based in Chesapeake, Virginia.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Michael C. Creedon Jr. | CEO & Director | Age 49 | Compensation: $5.3M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 150K people. Revenue per employee of $129K ranks in the bottom quartile among Consumer Defensive peers, with revenue growing 8%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $7K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $393 per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 41\u00d7 revenue per employee (elevated relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (7/10).","financial_summary":["Dollar Tree, Inc. is a large-cap consumer defensive business with a $25.9B market cap on $19.4B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -12% over the past three years and +2% annually across the full decade, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome.","Returns on capital run at 15% against a 9% cost of capital, a 7-point spread that creates value, though narrow enough that competitive pressure could close it, with gross margins sitting at 32%.","The business throws off $1.1B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 5 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.71\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.7\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage of 13\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Management returns a meaningful 6.0% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Revenue growth in the bottom decile of Consumer Defensive peers \u2014 losing share inside the profit pool","sev":"red","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Carries a 31% premium to sector median EV/EBITDA (confirmed by 1 other valuation measure)","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.3065981825365467},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 6.7% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $248.7M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Slow-motion taste and channel shifts \u2014 private label, Gen-Z health preferences, or a DTC disruption can erode decade-old brand moats without a single dramatic quarter.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Input cost inflation from agricultural commodities \u2014 when raw materials spike, pass-through pricing lags and margin compression is immediate.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"GLP-1 and health-trend demand destruction \u2014 weight-loss drugs and wellness shifts are structurally reducing consumption in snack, beverage, and packaged food categories.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Piotroski score 8/9 \u2014 financials improving across profitability, leverage and efficiency","Financial quality scores above most Consumer Defensive peers","Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment 